SINA_SMART["biophys1-radiobiology"] = {"lecture":"biophys1-radiobiology","title":"Radiobiology: biological effects of ionising radiation","mapStatus":"auto: generated by universal.py from the lecture summary and key points; not human-reviewed","los":[{"id":"biophys1-radiobiology:e0","text":"Effects depend on radiation, organism and dose/dose rate.","topic":"The big picture","sec":0,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"biophys1-radiobiology:e1","text":"Absorbed dose: Gy = J/kg (1 rad = 0.01 Gy).","topic":"Dose quantities","sec":1,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"biophys1-radiobiology:e2","text":"Equivalent dose H = D × W_R (Sv): photons, electrons 1; α 20.","topic":"Dose quantities","sec":1,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"biophys1-radiobiology:e3","text":"Effective dose E = Σ H_T × W_T (Sv).","topic":"Dose quantities","sec":1,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"biophys1-radiobiology:e4","text":"Water radiolysis → •OH, •H; oxygen increases damage.","topic":"Molecular and cellular effects","sec":2,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"biophys1-radiobiology:e5","text":"Double-strand breaks: most lethal; aberrations from 0.1 Gy.","topic":"Molecular and cellular effects","sec":2,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"biophys1-radiobiology:e6","text":"Death: necrosis, apoptosis (lymphocytes), mitotic death.","topic":"Molecular and cellular effects","sec":2,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"biophys1-radiobiology:e7","text":"Radiosensitive: marrow, gonads, mucosa, basal skin; M phase most, S phase least.","topic":"Molecular and cellular effects","sec":2,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"biophys1-radiobiology:e8","text":"Deterministic: threshold, severity ∝ dose. Stochastic: probability ∝ dose, no threshold.","topic":"Deterministic effects and acute irradiation","sec":3,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"biophys1-radiobiology:e9","text":"Acute: 1-2 Gy nausea; 2-4 Gy marrow; LD50 ≈ 4-4.5 Gy; > 6 Gy digestive.","topic":"Deterministic effects and acute irradiation","sec":3,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"}],"vmap":{},"mlevel":{"1lyb9jt":"a","k4cqp0":"a","dx81ca":"a","s7wz1j":"a","1a40mqy":"a","4wcojv":"a","n0vws9":"a","brctb0":"a","1mvo72y":"a","10dle91":"a","1pzx19k":"a","1ing89q":"a","8wtzw4":"a","7nr7lx":"a","qdwfg3":"a","ui5wb7":"a"},"mapCounts":{"verified":0,"auto":16,"ambiguous":0,"missing":0},"mrules":[],"items":[],"experimental":[],"status":{},"difficulty":{},"expl":{"biophys1-radiobiology:e0":{"s":0,"u":["Effects depend on radiation, organism and dose/dose rate.","Effects depend on the radiation (type, energy, path), the organism (cells, tissues, species) and the irradiation conditions (total dose, dose rate).","Radiobiology studies how ionising radiation acts on living organisms, from particles to disease, to understand radiotherapy and to ensure radiation protection ."],"dp":[]},"biophys1-radiobiology:e1":{"s":1,"u":["Absorbed dose: Gy = J/kg (1 rad = 0.01 Gy).","Absorbed dose D = energy per unit mass, in gray (Gy = J/kg) (old unit rad = 0.01 Gy); dose rate = dose per unit time."],"dp":[]},"biophys1-radiobiology:e2":{"s":1,"u":["Equivalent dose H = D × W_R (Sv): photons, electrons 1; α 20.","Equivalent dose H T = D × W R , in sievert (Sv) , weighting by radiation type: photons and electrons W R = 1 , protons 5, neutrons 5-20 by energy, α particles 20 ."],"dp":[]},"biophys1-radiobiology:e3":{"s":1,"u":["Effective dose E = Σ H_T × W_T (Sv).","Effective dose E = Σ H T × W T (Sv), weighting by tissue sensitivity; the lecture's table gives gonads 0.20; red marrow, colon, lung, stomach 0.12; bladder, liver, oesophagus, thyroid 0.04; skin, bone surface, brain, salivary glands 0.01; remainder 0.12."],"dp":[]},"biophys1-radiobiology:e4":{"s":2,"u":["Water radiolysis → •OH, •H; oxygen increases damage.","Water (~65% of body mass) is the main target: radiolysis yields •OH (strong oxidant) and •H ; high-LET radiation packs radicals densely (recombination, H₂O₂); oxygen forms more toxic species (O₂•⁻, ROO•) and raises radiosensitivity ; heat speeds radical diffusion."],"dp":[{"h":"Why oxygen makes tumours easier to kill","t":"Radiation breaks DNA directly and through radicals; oxygen reacts with the damaged sites and fixes the damage so it cannot be repaired. Well-oxygenated cells are therefore two to three times more radiosensitive than hypoxic ones. The poorly oxygenated core of large tumours resists radiotherapy, which is why treatment is split into fractions that let the tumour reoxygenate between sessions. Source: the lecture's free radical and oxygen slides."}]},"biophys1-radiobiology:e5":{"s":2,"u":["Double-strand breaks: most lethal; aberrations from 0.1 Gy.","DNA lesions : double-strand breaks (often lethal), single-strand breaks (usually repaired), base and sugar damage, cross-links; hits on oncogenes or tumour suppressors can start carcinogenesis.","Chromosomal aberrations appear from 0.1 Gy ."],"dp":[]},"biophys1-radiobiology:e6":{"s":2,"u":["Death: necrosis, apoptosis (lymphocytes), mitotic death.","Cell death : immediate necrosis at very high doses (10²-10³ Gy); apoptosis at lower doses in very sensitive cells (lymphocytes, oocytes); mitotic death (cells look normal but die when dividing); indirect death of differentiated cells from damage to their environment."],"dp":[]},"biophys1-radiobiology:e7":{"s":2,"u":["Radiosensitive: marrow, gonads, mucosa, basal skin; M phase most, S phase least.","Radiosensitivity is highest in rapidly renewing tissues (bone marrow, spermatogonia, mucosae, basal epidermis) and low in slowly renewing ones (liver, kidney, neurons: late effects); highest in M phase , lowest in S phase ; depends on oxygen and repair capacity (Bergonié-Tribondeau principle)."],"dp":[{"h":"Why the bone marrow fails first","t":"Tissues that constantly renew their cells depend on active divisions, and dividing cells are the most radiosensitive. After a few gray the marrow stem cells die; circulating blood cells then run out over the following weeks, giving infections and bleeding. Slowly renewing organs such as liver and brain show damage only much later. Source: the lecture's radiosensitivity and acute irradiation slides."}]},"biophys1-radiobiology:e8":{"s":3,"u":["Deterministic: threshold, severity ∝ dose. Stochastic: probability ∝ dose, no threshold.","Stochastic effects (cancer, hereditary mutations): probability, not severity, rises with dose, with no known threshold.","Deterministic effects : certain above a threshold dose , severity increasing with dose, due to cell death; early or medium-term, often reversible if limited."],"dp":[]},"biophys1-radiobiology:e9":{"s":3,"u":["Acute: 1-2 Gy nausea; 2-4 Gy marrow; LD50 ≈ 4-4.5 Gy; > 6 Gy digestive.","Acute whole-body irradiation : LD50 ≈ 4-4.5 Gy ); 6-7 Gy and above gastrointestinal syndrome (diarrhoea, vomiting, bleeding, dehydration), then neurological at very high doses."],"dp":[]}},"meta":{"1lyb9jt":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-radiobiology:e1/recall/1"},"k4cqp0":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-radiobiology:e2/recall/1"},"dx81ca":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-radiobiology:e2/recall/1"},"s7wz1j":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-radiobiology:e3/recall/1"},"1a40mqy":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-radiobiology:e4/recall/1"},"4wcojv":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-radiobiology:e4/recall/2"},"n0vws9":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-radiobiology:e4/recall/3"},"brctb0":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-radiobiology:e5/recall/1"},"1mvo72y":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-radiobiology:e5/recall/2"},"10dle91":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-radiobiology:e6/recall/1"},"1pzx19k":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-radiobiology:e7/recall/1"},"1ing89q":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-radiobiology:e6/recall/2"},"8wtzw4":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-radiobiology:e8/recall/1"},"7nr7lx":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-radiobiology:e8/recall/2"},"qdwfg3":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-radiobiology:e9/recall/1"},"ui5wb7":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-radiobiology:e9/recall/2"}},"explRev":"5ffac1e04933"};
